# A pair derived for the first time by a retraction. Assert `a1`, then `b1`
# with `b1.k == a1.k`, which blocks `(a1)` at the NOT node; then `c1` with
# `y = 0`, which the C node meets with no left token, so nothing traps.
# Retracting `b1` releases `(a1)` and derives `((a1), c1)` for the first time,
# and the division traps at the retraction: a trap is recorded each time the
# network derives the pair, and never for a pair a retraction ends.
#
# The division is a beta test, so `C c` is the third pattern ([IRET.6.2]
# admits no arithmetic in an alpha test). `x` and `y` are Int because only an
# all-Int division traps [LEXP.7.15.1].
use default

schema A scope logical:
    k: String key

schema B scope logical:
    k: String key

schema C scope logical:
    id: String key
    x: Int mutable
    y: Int mutable

rule r:
    A a
    no B b where b.k == a.k
    C c where c.x / c.y > 1.0
->
    log info "r fired"
